#### **3. Workflow and State Machine "Illegal Moves"**

CRM and Procurement apps rely on strict status flows (e.g., Draft → Pending → Approved → Paid).

**✅ The "Double-Submit" Race Condition**:
- Rapidly click **"Approve" twice** on a Grant application

**Defect Found**: In .NET, if not handled with thread safety or database locks, this can trigger **two separate payment disbursements** for one approval.

**Example test cases**:
```csharp
[Fact]
public async Task ApproveGrant_DoubleClick_OnlyProcessesOnce()
{
    var grantId = 123;
    
    // Simulate double-click: fire two approval requests simultaneously
    var task1 = _grantService.ApproveAsync(grantId);
    var task2 = _grantService.ApproveAsync(grantId);
    
    await Task.WhenAll(task1, task2);
    
    // Verify only ONE approval recorded
    var grant = await _grantService.GetByIdAsync(grantId);
    var approvalHistory = await _auditService.GetApprovalHistoryAsync(grantId);
    
    Assert.Single(approvalHistory); // Only one approval event
    Assert.Equal(GrantStatus.Approved, grant.Status);
}

[Fact]
public async Task ProcessPayment_DoubleSubmit_OnlyOnePaymentCreated()
{
    var paymentRequest = new PaymentRequest 
    { 
        Amount = 5000m,
        RecipientId = 456
    };
    
    // Simulate double-submit
    var task1 = _paymentService.ProcessAsync(paymentRequest);
    var task2 = _paymentService.ProcessAsync(paymentRequest);
    
    await Task.WhenAll(task1, task2);
    
    // Verify only ONE payment created in database
    var payments = await _paymentService.GetByRecipientAsync(456);
    var duplicates = payments.Where(p => p.Amount == 5000m).ToList();
    
    Assert.Single(duplicates); // Should be exactly 1, not 2
}
```

